      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "IL-4 mediates important proinflammatory functions in asthma, including induction of the IgE isotype switch, increased expression of vascular cell adhesion molecule 1 and promotion of eosinophil transmigration across the endothelium, stimulation of mucus production, and T(H)2 lymphocyte differentiation, leading to release of IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, and IL-13."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The current study evaluated the therapeutic potential of inhaled recombinant human soluble interleukin-4 receptor (IL-4R) as an IL-4 antagonist."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This study was a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study in 62 subjects involving 12 once weekly nebulizations of 0.75, 1.5, or 3.0 mg of IL-4R or placebo. During screening, subjects documented dependence on inhaled corticosteroids by an exacerbation in asthma induced by one or two 50% dose reductions at 2-week intervals. After restabilization for 2 weeks on the dose above which their asthma flared, inhaled steroids were discontinued, patients were randomized, and study medication was started on day 0."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "IL-4R was well tolerated. Efficacy was demonstrated by a decline in FEV(1) observed in the placebo group (-0.4 L and -13% predicted), which did not occur in the group receiving 3.0 mg of IL-4R (-0.1 L and -2% predicted; P =.05 over the 3-month treatment period). Daily patient-measured morning FEV(1) also demonstrated a significant decline in the placebo group (-0.5 L and -18% predicted), which did not occur in the group receiving 3.0 mg of IL-4R (-0.1 L and -4% predicted; P =.02 over the 3-month treatment period). The efficacy of IL-4R was further confirmed by the absence of increase in asthma symptom scores in the group receiving 3.0 mg of IL-4R (Delta 0.1) compared with that seen in the placebo group (Delta 1.4 over 1 month; P =.07). Study discontinuation for asthma exacerbation was not significantly different between groups (placebo, 56%; 3.0 mg of IL-4R, 47%; P = not significant)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These promising data suggest that IL-4R is safe and effective in the treatment of moderate persistent asthma."
        }
      ]},
